# Digilent Basys 3 (Xilinx XC7A35T-1CPG236C): `spi_console.v`.
#
# PROVENANCE: ball names transcribed from `examples/soc/board/basys3.rcf`,
# which transcribes Digilent's published `Basys-3-Master.xdc`. Two lines
# of that file have been CHECKED on this board and one of them was wrong,
# so treat the rest as a reading: A18/B18 are the FPGA's transmit and
# receive into the USB-UART bridge (CHECKED 8 October 2026 by echoing
# bytes through `uart_loop.v`), and `sw[0]`/`led[0]` are at the RIGHT-hand
# end (CHECKED the same day, correcting a comment that said left).
#
# ===================================================================
# WHERE TO CONNECT THE FOUR WIRES
# ===================================================================
#
# The top row of **Pmod JB**, plus a ground. Digilent numbers a Pmod
# connector 1-6 along the top row and 7-12 along the bottom, with pins 5
# and 11 ground and pins 6 and 12 3V3.
#
#   JB1   A14    sclk    the master's clock
#   JB2   A16    mosi    its data: commands and pixels on one wire
#   JB3   B15    dc      low for a command byte, high for a pixel byte
#   JB4   B16    cs_n    low while a byte is on the wire
#   JB5   —      GND     connect this: two boards need a common ground
#
# **All four are inputs.** This design never drives them; it is the
# display, not the driver.
#
# **3.3 V only.** The Basys 3's Pmod pins are 3.3 V CMOS. A 5 V master —
# a classic Arduino, for instance — needs level shifting, and connecting
# one directly risks the part.
#
# **The rate limit is a ratio**: `PHASE_MARGIN = 2` against a 100 MHz
# clock means each `sclk` phase must last at least two system clocks, so
# `sclk` may run up to 25 MHz. An SSD1306's own maximum is well under
# that. `overrun_count` rises before any bit is lost, so the counters say
# when the margin has gone rather than dropping bits quietly.
#
# JA and JC work identically — all three were built and routed on this
# part with zero unexplained bits, so the choice is physical convenience.
# To move, replace the four balls below with that header's top row:
#
#   JA   J1  L2  J2  G2         JC   K17 M18 N17 P18
#
# Avoid JXADC: it is the analog header and carries filtering that has no
# business on a digital clock.
#
# **NOT CHECKED: no signal has been driven into any Pmod on this board.**
# What the balls do is quoted from Digilent's master constraints, and what
# the flow does with them is verified only as far as "it places, routes
# and every bit decodes". The counters are the real test, and §4 of
# `ip/bus/spi_display_rx/README.md` says how to read them: both byte
# counts advancing while `bit_error_count` and `dc_change_count` stay at
# zero is a working link.
#
# LD6 (ball U14) is absent: it is the one LED ball of the sixteen in a
# `LIOB33_SING` tile, which this flow has no IO table for. A gap in the
# flow, not a dead ball. So `led[5]` is LD5 and `led[6]` is LD7.
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 clk W5

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 sclk A14
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 mosi A16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 dc   B15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 cs_n B16

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 uart_rx_pin B18
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 uart_tx_pin A18

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[0]  U16
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[1]  E19
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[2]  U19
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[3]  V19
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[4]  W18
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[5]  U15
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[6]  V14
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[7]  V13
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[8]  V3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[9]  W3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[10] U3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[11] P3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[12] N3
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[13] P1
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 led[14] L1

set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[0] W7
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[1] W6
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[2] U8
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[3] V8
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[4] U5
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[5] V5
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 seg[6] U7
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 dp     V7
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[0]  U2
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[1]  U4
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[2]  V4
set_io -io_standard LVCMOS33 an[3]  W4

create_clock -name sys -period 10.0 clk
clock_domain sys clk
